                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "future_plans",
                    "title": "be going to",
                    "explanation": "«Be going to» + verbo expresa planes e intenciones que ya hemos decidido:\n- «I'm going to book the tickets tonight.»\n\nTambién sirve para predicciones cuando vemos una prueba:\n- «Look at those clouds! It's going to rain.»\n\nNegativa y pregunta:\n- «I'm not going to walk all day.»\n- «Are we going to stay at Ciarán's flat? — Yes, we are. / No, we aren't.»\n\nExpresiones de futuro: «tonight», «tomorrow», «next weekend», «on Saturday».",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Sujeto",
                        "Afirmativa",
                        "Negativa",
                        "Pregunta"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "I",
                          "I'm going to book",
                          "I'm not going to book",
                          "Am I going to book?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "you / we / they",
                          "we're going to stay",
                          "we aren't going to stay",
                          "Are we going to stay?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "he / she / it",
                          "he's going to play",
                          "he isn't going to play",
                          "Is he going to play?"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "I'm going to book the train tickets tonight.",
                      "Where are we going to stay?",
                      "I'm not going to walk all day.",
                      "Look at those clouds! It's going to rain."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "future_plans",
                    "title": "Arrangements: present continuous · Shall we…?",
                    "explanation": "Para planes ya organizados (con día, hora o billetes) se usa mucho el present continuous con una expresión de futuro:\n- «We're going to London next weekend.»\n- «Sam's band is playing at a wedding on Saturday.»\n\nCompáralo con «be going to», que expresa una intención. Muchas veces valen los dos:\n- «I'm going to visit the British Museum.» (quiero, tengo pensado)\n\nPara hacer sugerencias u ofrecerse se usa «Shall I» / «Shall we» + verbo. Para contestar: «Good idea!», «Yes, please.», «No, thanks.»\n- «Shall we take the train?» (¿cogemos el tren?)\n- «Shall I book the tickets?» (¿reservo yo los billetes?)",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Uso",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Plan organizado",
                          "We're staying with Ciarán on Saturday."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Intención",
                          "I'm going to visit the British Museum."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Sugerencia",
                          "Shall we go to Camden Market?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Ofrecimiento",
                          "Shall I book the tickets tonight?"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "We're going to London next weekend.",
                      "Sam's band is playing at a wedding on Saturday.",
                      "Shall we take the Tube? — Good idea!",
                      "Shall I book the train tickets? — Yes, please."
                    ]
                  }
                },
